<div class="article-content entry-content">
		<article><div class="body" lang="en"><section id="sec1" class="sec"><p id="p-1">AI’s environmental impact is no longer theoretical: it is showing up in people’s lives. Across the U.S. and soon around the world, the rapid buildout of AI datacenters is driving up energy demand, draining water supplies, and reshaping communities, often without their consent. What was once framed as a technical scaling problem has become an environmental justice issue, playing out in zoning hearings, regulatory meetings, and angry town halls, as residents grapple with higher utility bills, constant noise, and contaminated or depleted water.</p><p id="p-2">The shift into public view has been swift. Viral videos, front-page headlines, and even local elections now center on the real costs of AI infrastructure—rising electricity rates, water scarcity, air pollution, and falling property values. The promised benefits of AI—helping people live better, healthier, more productive lives—remain largely unrealized, even as its environmental and social costs are imposed on communities with little say in the matter.</p><p id="p-3">Yet the industry’s response is depressingly familiar: promises of future fixes, vague assurances of community benefit, and ever more fantastic claims about what AI will someday deliver for everyone. In practice, those promises are often paired with aggressive accounting and legal maneuvers that obscure present-day harm: land quietly purchased through shell companies to avoid public scrutiny, or state and local officials required to sign nondisclosure agreements before they are even allowed to ask basic questions about water use, power demand, or emissions. We have at least three reasons to be skeptical of claims this AI infrastructure buildout will all be for the best.</p><p id="p-4">First, for more than a decade, a small but persistent community has documented computing’s—and especially AI’s—escalating climate and environmental impacts, arguing that unchecked growth would lead to precisely this moment.<a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B7" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B7"><sup>7</sup></a> <i>Communications</i> itself echoed that warning in the Opinion column “Our House Is on Fire,” rejecting “digital exceptionalism”—the belief that computing’s benefits justify unlimited expansion.<a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B5" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B5"><sup>5</sup></a></p><p id="p-5">Second, while AI is often promoted as a powerful tool for climate action—and it can be—its climate benefits must be weighed against both the direct costs of the infrastructure required to support it and the additional emissions enabled by AI-driven productivity gains, which recent modeling suggests may exceed the emissions AI helps avoid.<a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B1" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B1"><sup>1</sup></a> Investment and talent have largely shifted away from conservation and sustainability work toward scaling AI capabilities themselves. At the same time, the impacts that matter most to communities are systematically left out of industry reporting. Independent research from Hugging Face has shown that the metrics companies emphasize—energy use and greenhouse gas emissions—capture only a narrow slice of AI’s footprint, ignoring water extraction, land use, air pollution, labor impacts, and community displacement.<a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B6" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B6"><sup>6</sup></a> When these broader harms are accounted for, the picture changes dramatically: efficiency gains and AI-enabled climate tools are overshadowed by the physical and social costs of building and operating AI at scale.</p><p id="p-6">Third, the question is not whether AI can be useful—it can—but whether we can build it without replicating familiar patterns of environmental injustice, where benefits flow upward to a privileged few while harms are imposed locally and endure. Given the incentives shaping today’s tech industry, skepticism is warranted. As Cory Doctorow has argued, digital systems tend to follow a predictable path of enshittification: first optimized for growth, then for monetization, and ultimately for extraction at all costs.<a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B3" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B3"><sup>3</sup></a> That logic now governs AI infrastructure, where value is increasingly extracted not just from users as in the first Internet platforms (Facebook, Gmail, and so forth), but from land, water, air, and public resources. There is little reason to believe these systems will suddenly become more accountable simply because companies promise they will.</p><p id="p-8">The argument is this: the physical costs of AI need public governance. Who owns the infrastructure, who directs its growth, and who bears its costs cannot be left solely to private firms. Digital services are not immaterial: they are built from land, energy, labor, and water, or wai in the Hawaiian language. Treating AI as private infrastructure with public consequences is no longer tenable if AI is meant to deliver shared prosperity. AI must be governed as part of the public trust: the principle that essential shared resources are held in stewardship for the public, protected from exclusive private capture, and managed for present and future generations. For AI infrastructure, that means transparency about water, energy, land, and emissions; requiring large users to bear the costs they impose on public infrastructure; meaningful community authority over siting and resource use; and public or community-controlled alternatives where critical infrastructure has become too concentrated. This idea is an ancient one. In Hawaii, Kānāwai—water law—recognized water as a communal trust to ensure all people could grow food, with dire consequences if someone took more than a fair share. Achieving this shift requires more than optimization. Computing professionals must build and defend new technical and institutional alternatives—free and open source systems, repairable and maintainable hardware, distributed and community-controlled infrastructure, and mechanisms that align power with accountability—while standing alongside Indigenous and frontline communities as leaders in governance. I tell the story of wai through two stories from my home in Atlanta, GA, and my homeland of Hawaii, because they reveal what is already unfolding everywhere and offer us historical and current perspectives on the impacts of privatizing AI infrastructure. This moment of attention is an opportunity we must seize to determine AI, and our, future.</p></section><section id="sec2" class="sec"><h2 class="heading">Your City: The Next Datacenter Megalopolis</h2><p id="p-9">During the past five years, the U.S. has experienced an unprecedented datacenter buildout, foreshadowing the global expansion now underway. In my home in Atlanta, GA, and the surrounding metropolitan area, we are seeing it in a transformative way. With approximately 1.46GW of datacenter capacity built, and another 2.08GW under construction, concerns about noise, property values, water availability, and power rates are on the minds of many. These developments carry real public health risks, including degraded air quality that disproportionately affects people with asthma.<a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B11" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B11"><sup>11</sup></a> In nearby Newton County, GA, national attention followed reports of household wells running dry and tap water turning brown after a new Meta datacenter came online (Meta disputes they caused well problems).</p><p id="p-10">Communities have not been passive. Neighborhood planning units, local academics, and environmental groups have pressed city and state leaders to require datacenter developers to pay for the power infrastructure they need, rather than shifting those costs onto residents. Advocates have pushed for higher electricity rates for datacenters as large industrial consumers and have successfully blocked new facilities near dense neighborhoods and sensitive watersheds. Consumer groups such as the People’s Power Union and Science4GA have challenged the outsized lobbying influence of Georgia’s energy sector after years of rate hikes. Legislative victories, however, have been elusive: a bill to increase energy payments from new datacenters stalled despite broad support, while another to suspend generous tax breaks was vetoed by the governor.</p><p id="p-11">The most consequential decision came in December 2025, when the Georgia Public Service Commission approved an unprecedented $15 billion, nearly 10,000-megawatt expansion of power infrastructure to meet projected AI and datacenter demand. The vote occurred during the final days of a lame-duck commission—after voters had ousted two incumbent commissioners over affordability and energy concerns in a nationally covered election with record-breaking voter turnout, but before their replacements could be seated—leaving ratepayers potentially on the hook for decades of higher costs and increased climate burden. Anger has been palpable. Since 2023, the commission has approved six Georgia Power rate increases, raising the average household bill by at least $43 a month, or roughly $500 a year, even as the company’s profits rose by 40 percent. For many Georgians, the message is clear: the benefits of AI infrastructure flow elsewhere, while the costs are being paid at home.</p><p id="p-12">First it was Northern Virginia, then Atlanta, and now the buildout is global. Singapore, despite its challenging climate and limited land, is converting land for datacenter use; in Southern Italy, a “Mediterranean AI Hub” is being developed; and in India, Microsoft and others are investing billions in new datacenters. The scale of this expansion is staggering: the IEA projects global datacenter electricity consumption to nearly double, from approximately 485TWh in 2025 to approximately 950TWh by 2030. Across these locations, consumer and environmental advocates are refusing to let Big Tech treat climate responsibility as a branding exercise or a series of accounting tricks. Efficiency improvements often trigger rebound effects: when a technology becomes more efficient and cheaper to use, total consumption tends to increase rather than decrease, ultimately exacerbating resource demand and environmental impact.<a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B9" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B9"><sup>9</sup></a> In the context of AI and datacenters, making computing “greener” without limiting overall growth simply accelerates energy, water, and land use rather than reducing harm, even when offset by credits, which are often fraudulent.<a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B10" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B10"><sup>10</sup></a> For many Georgia residents, these were abstract connections. Now, we are seeing in real time how our own water, land, and tax dollars are being used to support AI use everywhere except here. If allowed to continue, we as individuals, as communities, as parents, as employees, as renters, as homeowners, as citizens, will see our lives negatively impacted in the near term.</p></section><section id="sec3" class="sec"><h2 class="heading">What Can We Do?</h2><p id="p-13">Data Center Watch, a research project from 10a Labs that tracks grassroots opposition to datacenter development across the U.S., reported that by early 2026 more than 800 active opposition groups were organizing across 49 states, with approximately $130 billion in datacenter projects blocked or delayed in the first quarter of 2026 alone through community meetings, classrooms, local policymaking, and activism led by those who often bear the brunt of climate impacts: Indigenous communities, black communities, and other communities of color. It is clear that communities are pushing back, and we should be paying attention.</p><p id="p-14">Our key question is how we, as computing professionals, support this grassroots effort. I take hope from people already using computing in radically different ways—practitioners working alongside environmental justice groups, leveraging technology not for extractive industry goals but for genuine public good. This is not a new fight: decades ago, communities pushed back against landfills being placed only in black neighborhoods, and now we are seeing similar battles play out over the placement of datacenters and the draining of local resources. Meaningful change requires collective action that moves beyond technical optimization and brings together consumers, policymakers, and community organizations to force industry shifts. What is needed now are morally ambitious people willing to steer the direction of computing and AI at every level. So what can be done? Several existing efforts point toward what this can look like:</p><ul class="list" data-jats-list-type="bullet"><li class="list-item"><p id="p-15"><b>Community-governed, place-based computing for stewardship</b>. Direct partnerships such as the STRONG Manoomin Collective in the Great Lakes region and the CEAR Hub in Brunswick, GA, show what community-governed, place-based AI can look like in practice. Working alongside federally recognized Ojibwe tribes, STRONG Manoomin develops sensing and AI systems to preserve wetlands and assert tribal sovereignty, utilizing data for stewardship, advocacy, and cultural restoration, rather than as benchmarks.<a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B8" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B8"><sup>8</sup></a> In Brunswick, youth and researchers co-deploy water-level sensors, climate curriculum, and flood modeling in neighborhoods experiencing rising flood risk. In both cases, computing is embedded in local knowledge and lived experience, serving community resilience rather than extractive innovation.</p></li><li class="list-item"><p id="p-16"><b>Indigenous-led technology and ancestral stewardship.</b> In Hawaii, the Purple Mai’a Foundation works at the intersection of Indigenous knowledge, computing, and entrepreneurship, co-developing technologies with Native Hawaiian youth and communities to restore and protect ecosystems. Their programs train a new generation of technologists grounded in ancestral stewardship. The Nation of Hawaii, too, leverages sensors, mapping tools, and networked infrastructure to monitor and protect āina (land) from overdevelopment. At a recent event, called Build4’Āina, computing professionals from Big Tech companies, academic computing researchers, and local artists and practitioners gathered to build AI and sensor systems to directly support community conservation, traditional food production, and community action, eventually demoing these systems to state government agencies.</p></li><li class="list-item"><p id="p-17"><b>Carbon transparency and literacy for developers:</b> Tools such as GreenPT and CodeCarbon can help developers see the resource costs of AI workloads, challenging the illusion that computing is zero-impact.</p></li></ul><p id="p-18">These efforts need more hands—now. Every computing professional has a role to play in shifting AI from extractive infrastructure to community-governed systems, tools for resilience, land stewardship, and self-determination, not harm.</p></section><section id="sec4" class="sec"><h2 class="heading">Lessons from Hawaii’s past for AI’s Future</h2><p id="p-19">I conclude with a warning from the past, and a vision of hope for our shared future. My people (Native Hawaiians) have a proverb taught to almost every child: <i>I Ka Wā Ma Mua, I Ka Wā Ma Hope</i>—the future is in the past. Other indigenous cultures describe this as moving backward into the future—the belief that past experiences, traditions, ancestral knowledge, and learning will guide us today in making forward-looking and just decisions.</p><p id="p-20">My Polynesian ancestors voyaged across the Pacific to the Hawaiian islands more than 1,000 years ago and built extraordinary, whole-island wai (the word for freshwater, the most important resource on an island surrounded by ocean) engineering works called ahupua’a to sustain themselves. They developed a system of laws called Kānāwai, which ensured a careful, place-based framework for food production, water protection, and people’s well-being. The fresh water stream was life, and wealth—called waiwai, as in much water—was measured not in extraction or accumulation, but in the ability of water to support everyone. At its peak, this system supported more than 700,000 people’s needs, while today, most food is imported to Hawaii, and the Hawaiian islands and their people are at extraordinary risk from climate degradation.</p><p id="p-21">The story of Hawaii’s transformation from a self-sufficient, 1,000-year-old advanced sovereign nation to a subjugated state with the highest cost of living in the U.S., and dire food and water insecurity, is a complex one. However, one particular period in the Hawaiian Kingdom’s history mirrors our current time.</p><p id="p-22">Water as a public trust resource was codified in written law in 1840. Even as sugar plantations expanded and profits from foreign investors overrode local concerns, systems were in place to regulate water withdrawals (via large ditches and built infrastructure) to ensure that small farmers could access water alongside the enormous sugar plantations. Water commissioners from the region were tasked with weighing impacts, scheduling withdrawal times, and preventing total enclosure of resources. The system was rooted in Hawaiian values that emphasized the perpetuation of land and water for future generations, as well as the local knowledge that the water cycle influenced everything from wildfire frequency to fisheries and lei making. Disruption would harm all; water was a public trust, not a commodity. My own ancestors were part of the commissioning body that adjudicated water management at the time, listening to oral testimony from farmers, plantation representatives, and long-time residents, to ensure fair use. Often, the stream diversions benefited small farmers, who could take advantage of the large infrastructure projects undertaken by sugar plantations. The Hawaiian kingdom leveraged revenue from sugar plantations during this time to build public works, educate the population, pay for roads, schools, and hospitals, ensuring the public trust.<a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B2" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B2"><sup>2</sup></a></p><p id="p-23">This robust public trust framework collapsed half a century later with the overthrow of the monarchy by sugar plantation leaders supported by the U.S. Marines, eager to exploit a fraught time in global history, and ride industrialization to drive huge profits. The consolidation of rights over nearly all water on the islands into the hands of “The Big Five,” descendants of missionary families and other businessmen, concentrated in corporations, wrested control away from community stewardship. Sugar production skyrocketed, and water became scarce (it takes approximately 1,000 liters of water to produce one pound of sugar). By the time plantations eventually shut down, well into the 20<sup>th</sup> century, the water and land were already broken. Today, Hawaii imports most of its food, and the legacy of diverted streams still haunts the islands.</p><p id="p-24">I see in this history a warning, but also a guide for how we govern AI. Hawaii’s Big Five consolidated the infrastructure of sugar—the land, water, finance, and transportation on which the industry depended—and with it, the power to redirect shared resources toward private profit. Today’s hyperscalers are consolidating the infrastructure through which AI is built and delivered. At the end of 2025, hyperscale operators controlled nearly half of global datacenter capacity, and Amazon, Microsoft, and Google alone accounted for roughly three-fifths of hyperscale capacity. The danger is not concentration alone, but what control of infrastructure allows its owners to control upstream: access to energy, water, land, public investment, and ultimately the terms under which communities participate in the AI economy. As that control concentrates, decisions about shared resources move away from the communities that bear their consequences. Just as plantations diverted streams to maximize production while while leaving communities dry, today’s tech giants today’s tech giants divert energy, water, and public resources to feed their own system. If we build AI the way sugar captured water—extractive, consolidated, and dismissive of community voice—we will inherit the same legacies of scarcity, inequity, and disempowerment. But if we treat AI like wai, belonging to the public trust of land, water, and air, we can chart a different course.</p><p id="p-25">British-American computer scientist and technology entrepreneur Andrew Ng said, “AI is the new electricity.” I disagree. AI is water, AI is wai. Literally, because we transform water into computation in datacenters, and figuratively, because AI, like water, should be a public trust resource, available to all. In Hawaiian, waiwai means both water and true wealth. AI can become waiwai—shared abundance. To avoid repeating Hawaii’s history, we as computing professionals must empower and collaborate with communities, especially indigenous ones, who have safeguarded water and other vital resources for generations, reimagining these systems, harnessing this extraordinary moment of consciousness about AI’s impacts, to build a better future for us all.</p></section><section id="sec5" class="sec"></section></div><footer class="back"></footer></article>
</div>
